-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Eviction Policy: 2Q
+
+The cache uses 2Q with two FIFO queues:
+
+- **A1 (first-use queue)** — holds a sheet after its first render or a fresh render. When A1 exceeds its size cap, the sheet at A1's front is evicted.
+- **Am (main queue)** — holds sheets that were reused at least once. A hit in Am moves the sheet to Am's most-recently-used end. When the total cache exceeds 12 GiB, the sheet at Am's least-recently-used end is evicted first.
+
+On a cache hit in A1, the sheet is promoted to Am (an entry may hold no duplicate across queues). Eviction order: first drain A1 down to its cap, then evict from Am's least-recently-used end until the total is within 12 GiB. A promoted sheet that leaves Am through eviction is treated as first-use again on its next miss and re-enters A1.
+
+The size cap on A1 is the tunable parameter that shields comparison sheets from one-off screening scans: it starts at 25% of the budget (3 GiB, roughly 1,500 sheets at a 2 MiB sheet size) and is tuned against observed screening behavior after the pilot. Policy bookkeeping — both queues, hit accounting, and the cap — lives exclusively inside the worker; no other node observes it.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q with an A1/Am queue split | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Comparison sessions repeatedly revisit a small working set while screenings introduce long one-off scans. 2Q protects reused comparison sheets from scan pollution; the accepted cost is two queues and an A1 size cap to tune. LRU adapts fast but a single long sequential screening can evict useful comparison sheets. Aged frequency counts retain popular clips but add counter and aging maintenance and can keep a prior project's favorites warm after a switch. All three policies leave the external interface, queue concurrency, and disk budget unchanged, so the choice was internal cost versus scan resistance. |
+
